ng  us we know the lineage of every single  cell in us watch each cell is and what  its gonna become so so you have this  tool on one hand and on the other hand  we also collaborators he see Ron and I  with another scientist he said  microscopist at the nih called harish  rough and hairy developed a microscope  that was much more powerful than any  microscope that existed at the time and  allowed us to image the embryo  continuously and seamlessly for long  periods of time without damage in the  embryo that work was very important and  in combination with these computer tools  allow us to do things that we couldn't  do before so here's the microscope at  work looking  also the development of this embryo but  you can see is happening much faster  because this microscope that Harry  developed is 30 times faster than the  microscopes that we had before so that  all of a sudden by linking our brains we  were able to do more than we could have  each done individually and this is  actually very important because this is  how science is done at all levels even  if you're claiming to be a scientist  that you were completely alone you're  actually you're you're receiving  knowledge from other colleagues that are  actually colleagues that could be  contemporaries or it could be colleagues  that passed away that generated  knowledge before you came along and  you're building on that knowledge so in  that sense science is actually a network  of networks and it's a community where  we're continuously building on other  people's knowledge and in our case here  we were able to then link what's here on  Wall Street what I was doing and what  Harry was doing to then generate videos  like the one that I'm showing you here  of an embryo that is developing in in  real time and you can see the neurons in  green you can see the nuclei in red and  all of a saw and what this embryo starts  moving you can see that we can actually  keep track of the individual cells  because the microscope that Harry  developed is so efficient and so fast  and we can observe neurodevelopmental  events that were previously inaccessible  and in that way answer questions that we  couldn't answer before so I hope that  with these examples I have Illustrated  to you a little bit how science works  how collaborations work the importance  of collaborations in Sciences which is  fundamental but seldom discussed and if  you're interested in these topics and  you want to hear more I invite you to do  a Google search of my last name with  that block I have a blog on this topic  and also a TEDx talk that discusses in  more depth how the importance of basic  research in biomedicine thank you  you
 

